/* Technician's explanations: the longer text behind "Explain this more", one per question id. Loaded by app.js after the first screen. */
window.EXPLAIN={
"T0A01":"A 12-volt battery won't shock you through dry hands, but it can deliver huge current. Shorting its terminals, such as with a wrench, can cause burns, fire or even an explosion.\n\nRemove rings and watches, and keep tools away from the terminals.",
"T0A02":"Current flowing through the body can heat tissue, disrupt the electrical function of cells (including the heart), and cause involuntary muscle contractions, so you can't let go.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T0A03":"In US AC wiring, black is hot, carrying the voltage. White is neutral, and green or bare copper is ground.\n\nNever assume; turn off the breaker and test before working.",
"T0A04":"A fuse removes power when there's an overload. When too much current flows, it melts and opens the circuit.\n\nThat protects equipment and wiring and prevents fires. It won't protect you from shock: a fuse needs amps to blow, and a few hundredths of an amp through your chest can kill.",
"T0A05":"A fuse protects the wiring. A 5-amp circuit's wiring isn't designed for 20 amps.\n\nWith a 20-amp fuse, enough current could flow to overheat wiring and start a fire before the fuse blows. Always replace with the same rating.",
"T0A06":"Use three-wire cords and plugs, connect all station equipment to a common safety ground, and discharge capacitors before working on equipment. The third, ground wire gives a fault a safe path, so the breaker trips instead of a metal case becoming live.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T0A07":"A lightning arrester belongs on a grounded panel near where feed lines enter the building.\n\nThat sends lightning energy to ground before it gets inside. At the radio would be too late: the surge would already be in the house.",
"T0A08":"A fuse or circuit breaker is installed in series with the hot conductor only. In series means all the current must pass through it, so when it opens, the power stops.\n\nNever fuse the neutral or ground, which could leave equipment energized with the fuse blown.",
"T0A09":"All external ground rods and earth connections should be bonded together with heavy wire or conductive strap.\n\nThat keeps them at the same potential during a lightning strike, preventing dangerous voltage differences.",
"T0A10":"Charging or discharging an unprotected battery too quickly can cause overheating or out-gassing, venting flammable gases.\n\nLead-acid batteries release hydrogen. Lithium batteries can catch fire.",
"T0A11":"Power supply filter capacitors store a charge and can hold a dangerous voltage after the supply is turned off.\n\nThat charge can stay even with the cord unplugged. Always discharge them safely before working inside.",
"T0A12":"When measuring high voltage, use a voltmeter and test leads rated for that voltage.\n\nUnderrated equipment can arc, fail or shock you.",
"T0B01":"Lightning ground wires should be short and direct. Long wires and sharp bends raise impedance and can cause lightning to jump elsewhere.\n\nA drip loop or right-angle bends would do the opposite, so keep the path straight and short.",
"T0B02":"Climbing a tower requires training, an approved climbing harness and staying tied off at all times.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T0B03":"Never climb a tower without a helper or observer on the ground.\n\nIf something goes wrong, someone needs to be there to help.",
"T0B04":"When putting up a tower or antenna, look for and stay clear of overhead power lines.\n\nContact with a power line can be fatal. The other choices are wrong: towers are grounded, not insulated, and a wrist strap is for static-sensitive electronics, not towers.",
"T0B05":"A safety wire through a turnbuckle keeps vibration from slowly loosening it.\n\nLoose guy lines could let the tower fall.",
"T0B06":"Place antennas so that if they fall, no part can come within 10 feet of power lines.\n\nPlan for the worst case.",
"T0B07":"Never climb a crank-up tower unless it's fully retracted or mechanical safety locks are installed.\n\nThe cables or mechanism could fail and drop the sections.",
"T0B08":"For a tower, each leg should have its own 8-foot ground rod, bonded to the tower and to each other.\n\nThat spreads the lightning energy into the earth.",
"T0B09":"Utility poles carry high-voltage power lines. Attaching an antenna to one risks contact with those lines.\n\nIt's also usually prohibited by the utility.",
"T0B10":"Lightning ground conductors should avoid sharp bends. Lightning's fast current resists sudden turns and can jump off the wire.\n\nUse gentle curves.",
"T0B11":"Local electrical codes set grounding requirements for towers and antennas.\n\nThe FCC doesn't set these rules. Check your local codes.",
"T0C01":"Radio signals are non-ionizing radiation. They don't have enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms or break chemical bonds.\n\nX-rays and gamma rays are ionizing.",
"T0C02":"The strictest RF exposure limits fall between 30 and 300 MHz, where the human body absorbs RF most efficiently. There an adult is roughly half a wavelength tall, so the body soaks up energy like an antenna.\n\nOf the choices, 50 MHz has the lowest maximum permissible exposure (MPE).",
"T0C03":"Duty cycle is the percentage of time a transmitter is on. If it drops from 100 to 50 percent, the average exposure halves.\n\nSo the allowable power density doubles, increasing by a factor of 2.",
"T0C04":"RF exposure depends on frequency and power, distance from the antenna and the antenna's radiation pattern.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T0C05":"The human body absorbs more RF energy at some frequencies than at others, especially around 30 to 300 MHz.\n\nSo exposure limits vary with frequency. It isn't that lower frequencies carry more energy; if anything, the reverse is true.",
"T0C06":"To check compliance with RF exposure rules, you can use calculations from FCC OET Bulletin 65, computer modeling, or measurements with calibrated equipment.\n\nAll of the choices are acceptable.",
"T0C07":"Touching an antenna while it transmits can cause an RF burn to the skin.\n\nRF current flows through you at the contact point, heating tissue.",
"T0C08":"Moving antennas away from people reduces RF exposure.\n\nThe antenna is the source; moving the transmitter doesn't change exposure. Raising the duty cycle would increase it.",
"T0C09":"Re-evaluate your station whenever you change anything in the transmitter or antenna system.\n\nNew power levels, antennas or locations can change exposure.",
"T0C10":"Duty cycle affects average RF exposure. A transmitter on less of the time causes less average exposure.\n\nThat's why it's a factor in exposure calculations.",
"T0C11":"Duty cycle is the percentage of time a transmitter is transmitting during the averaging period.\n\nCW and SSB voice have lower duty cycles than FM and many digital modes, which keep the transmitter at full power the whole time you're keyed.",
"T0C12":"RF radiation doesn't have enough energy to cause chemical changes in cells or damage DNA.\n\nIonizing radiation, like X-rays and gamma rays, does.\n\nThat doesn't make RF perfectly safe: strong RF fields heat body tissue, which is why exposure limits exist.",
"T0C13":"The station licensee is responsible for ensuring no one is exposed to RF above FCC limits.\n\nThat includes neighbors and visitors.",
"T1A01":"Part 97.1 gives five reasons the amateur service exists: emergency communication, advancing the radio art, improving technical and communication skills, a reserve of trained operators and technicians, and international goodwill.\n\nThe wrong answers describe other things. Radio for anyone who wants it is the Citizens Band idea, and contests are popular but not a stated purpose. If an answer sounds like skill-building or public service, it is probably the right one.",
"T1A02":"The Federal Communications Commission writes the Part 97 rules and enforces them, including fines and license actions. Its rules come from US law and international treaties.\n\nThe ARRL is the national association of hams. It lobbies, publishes and runs volunteer examiner programs, but it has no legal power over anyone. Remember: FCC makes the rules, the ARRL helps hams live with them.",
"T1A03":"A phonetic alphabet replaces letters with words (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie) so a call sign survives noise and accents. On phone, the FCC encourages it but does not require it.\n\nThe rule only requires that a voice ID be in English. Phonetics are a recommended aid, not required for emergency messages or foreign contacts either. Nobody gets cited for saying \"K F one\" instead of \"Kilo Foxtrot One.\"",
"T1A04":"The FCC stopped mailing paper licenses years ago. Once your exam results are processed, it emails you a link to download your official license grant as a PDF.\n\nThat makes keeping a working email address on file with the FCC important, which shows up again in question T1C04. Check your spam folder after an exam session.",
"T1A05":"The FCC's Universal Licensing System (ULS) is the official record. Your license exists, legally, once it appears there with your call sign.\n\nThe Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination (CSCE) your examiners hand you only proves you passed. It matters for upgrades, not for going on the air as a new ham. Think: the database is the license.",
"T1A06":"Propagation beacons are automatic transmitters that send a call sign and short message all day. Hearing one tells you the band is open to the beacon's location.\n\nOn HF, the rules allow automatically controlled beacons only between 28.200 and 28.300 MHz on 10 meters. That slice sits just below the Technician phone segment that starts at 28.300 MHz, which makes it easy to picture.",
"T1A07":"Part 97 defines a space station purely by altitude: any amateur station more than 50 km above the Earth's surface. That includes satellites, the ISS amateur station and even a high-altitude rocket while it is up there.\n\nThe number to remember is 50 km. Balloons usually top out around 30 to 40 km, so they don't count.",
"T1A08":"Repeaters need coordinated channels so two machines in the same area don't step on each other. A Volunteer Frequency Coordinator keeps the records and recommends input and output pairs.\n\nThe FCC doesn't assign repeater frequencies, and coordination isn't strictly required, but coordinated repeaters get preference if interference disputes arise. The key word is volunteer.",
"T1A09":"Frequency coordinators are chosen from the bottom up. The amateurs in a local or regional area whose stations are eligible to be repeater or auxiliary stations pick the coordinator for their region.\n\nNeither the FCC nor the ARRL appoints them. It's self-governance, matching the volunteer spirit of the service.",
"T1A10":"RACES, the Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service, lets hams support government emergency management. Because RACES works under civil defense authorities, a license alone isn't enough.\n\nThe control operator must also be certified as currently enrolled by a civil defense organization. Think of it as a ham license plus a badge from the emergency agency.",
"T1A11":"Willful or malicious interference means deliberately disrupting other stations, and Part 97 bans it outright. It's one of the most serious violations and can cost you your license and bring heavy fines.\n\nThe other choices are permitted activities. VHF contacts with foreign hams and third-party traffic over digital modes are both normal amateur operating.",
"T1B01":"On 10 meters, Technicians may use phone (voice) between 28.300 and 28.500 MHz, with up to 200 watts PEP. They also have CW and data from 28.000 to 28.300 MHz.\n\nThis 200 kHz voice window is the Technician's main taste of HF voice, and when the band is open it can reach around the world. Picture 28.3 to 28.5 as \"the Tech phone slice.\"",
"T1B02":"The ISS carries amateur gear, and any US ham with a Technician license or higher may contact it on VHF and UHF. No special NASA permission is needed.\n\nThe usual frequencies are around 145.800 MHz for downlink voice. Contacts last only a few minutes during each pass, since the station moves fast across the sky.",
"T1B03":"The 6-meter band runs from 50 to 54 MHz. Of the choices, only 52.525 MHz falls inside, and it happens to be the national 6-meter FM simplex calling frequency.\n\nA handy check: the wavelength in meters is about 300 divided by the frequency in MHz. 300 / 52.5 is about 5.7 meters, close to 6.",
"T1B04":"Two meters covers 144 to 148 MHz, and 146.52 MHz sits right in the middle. It's the national FM simplex calling frequency, where hams call each other without a repeater.\n\nWavelength check: 300 / 146 is about 2 meters. Many new hams program 146.52 into their handhelds first, so it's worth memorizing.",
"T1B05":"FT8 is a popular weak-signal digital mode. Technicians have data privileges on part of 10 meters (28.000 to 28.300 MHz), and on 6 and 2 meters everywhere except the CW-only bottom 100 kHz, so they can run FT8 on all three.\n\nWhen \"All these choices are correct\" appears with three bands Technicians clearly have, it's usually the answer.",
"T1B06":"Technicians have HF privileges on 80, 40, 15 and 10 meters, but on the first three they're limited to CW. Voice on HF is allowed only on 10 meters, from 28.300 to 28.500 MHz.\n\nThe General license is what opens voice on the other HF bands. For the exam, link \"Technician HF phone\" to \"10 meters only.\"",
"T1B07":"The bottom 100 kHz of both 6 and 2 meters is reserved for CW (Morse code). That's 50.0 to 50.1 MHz and 144.0 to 144.1 MHz.\n\nWeak-signal operators use these segments for long-distance CW contacts and beacons. Only 6 and 2 meters have a CW-only slice at the bottom. The other choices are traps: 219 to 220 MHz is for data, and the 420 and 902 MHz bands allow many modes right from their lower edge.",
"T1B08":"Some amateur bands are shared with other radio services. Where amateurs are secondary, the primary users come first.\n\nYou may hear non-amateur stations there, and you must not cause them harmful interference. If they interfere with you, you have to put up with it. Secondary means second in line.",
"T1B09":"Your radio's frequency display may not be perfectly accurate, oscillators can drift as they warm up, and every signal has sidebands that extend above and below the dial frequency.\n\nAny of those can push part of your signal outside the band, which is a violation. Keep your transmissions comfortably inside the edges, especially on SSB, which is about 3 kHz wide.",
"T1B10":"Above 50 MHz, every amateur band includes at least one segment where SSB voice is allowed. On 6 and 2 meters that is everything above the CW-only bottom 100 kHz, and on the higher bands phone is allowed nearly everywhere (the 219 to 220 MHz data segment is the exception). By band-plan custom, SSB activity on 6 and 2 meters gathers near the low end, around 50.125 and 144.200 MHz.\n\nSSB on VHF and UHF is used for weak-signal and long-distance contacts, while FM is used for local repeater chat.",
"T1B11":"In their HF segments (80, 40, 15 and 10 meters), Technicians may run up to 200 watts PEP. PEP, peak envelope power, is the highest power at the crest of the signal.\n\nAbove 30 MHz the limit jumps to the full 1,500 watts. Remember: HF 200, VHF and up 1,500.",
"T1B12":"Above 30 MHz, Technicians have the same 1,500 watts PEP legal limit as every other license class, except in a few specific restricted segments.\n\nThat high limit is rarely needed. Good practice is always to use the minimum power necessary to make the contact.",
"T1C01":"Today the FCC issues new licenses in only three classes: Technician, General and Amateur Extra.\n\nNovice and Advanced licenses still exist for people who already hold them and keep renewing, but no one can earn them new. Technician Plus is gone as a class: those licenses are renewed as Technician. The three-step ladder is Technician, General, Extra.",
"T1C02":"Any licensed amateur may apply for a vanity call sign, a call sign you choose rather than one assigned in sequence.\n\nYour license class decides which formats you may pick. Technicians can choose from Group C and D formats, while Extras can get the short, prized 1x2 and 2x1 calls.",
"T1C03":"International ham contacts must stay within the spirit of the service: talk about amateur radio itself, or personal remarks like weather, family and hobbies.\n\nBusiness, advertising and anything for profit are out. Think of it as a friendly chat between hobbyists across borders.",
"T1C04":"The FCC communicates with licensees by email, so you must keep a current address on file in ULS.\n\nIf the FCC can't reach you, it may revoke your station license or suspend your operator license. Update your email whenever it changes, just as you would a mailing address.",
"T1C05":"New Technicians get a Group D call sign. The format is two letters, one digit, then three letters, like KF1XXX.\n\nThe digit is a call area number. Shorter formats depend on license class. Technicians may also hold Group C calls (one letter, a digit, three letters, like N1XYZ), but the sequential supply ran out long ago. Group B calls, like KB1AB, need an Advanced or Extra license, and Group A calls, like K1AB or KA1X, are for Extras only.",
"T1C06":"In international waters, you may operate your amateur station aboard a vessel documented (registered) in the United States, as long as the ship's master gives permission.\n\nYour FCC license works there because a vessel documented in the US is under US rules, even far from shore. On a foreign-flagged vessel, different rules apply.",
"T1C07":"You can apply to renew your license up to 90 days before it expires. That gives you time to handle any problems with the application.\n\nRenewal is done online through the FCC's ULS. Numbers for licenses: 10 years long, renew in the last 90 days, 2 years of grace afterward.",
"T1C08":"An amateur license lasts ten years. Renewal resets the clock for another ten.\n\nMost hams renew several times over their lives without ever retaking an exam. Pair the facts: ten-year term, 90-day renewal window, two-year grace period.",
"T1C09":"If your license expires, you have two years to renew it without retaking the exam.\n\nDuring that grace period your license is expired, so you can't transmit (see T1C11). After two years it's gone, and you'd have to test again to get a new one.",
"T1C10":"You may transmit as soon as your license grant shows up in the FCC's ULS database. Passing the exam alone doesn't let you transmit.\n\nThis usually takes a few days to a couple of weeks after your exam session. Watch ULS for your call sign to appear.",
"T1C11":"During the two-year grace period, the license is expired. You may renew it, but you can't transmit until the renewal is granted and shows in the database.\n\nThe grace period protects your call sign and spares you the exam, not your operating privileges.",
"T1D01":"The International Telecommunication Union (ITU) coordinates radio worldwide. If a country's government tells the ITU it objects to amateur contacts with its stations, FCC-licensed hams must not contact them.\n\nThe ARRL and IARU are amateur clubs and can't ban anything. Look for the answer that names the international treaty body.",
"T1D02":"Amateur radio is two-way communication between hams, so broadcasting to the general public is prohibited.\n\nSome one-way transmissions are allowed: code practice, telemetry, beacons, and brief announcements of interest to other hams. The test: is it meant for the public at large? Then it's broadcasting.",
"T1D03":"Amateur radio is meant to be open. Messages encoded to hide their meaning are generally forbidden.\n\nThe narrow exception is control commands to space stations or to radio-controlled model craft, where secrecy keeps outsiders from taking over the device. Standard digital modes aren't encryption, since their formats are published.",
"T1D04":"Music is normally prohibited on the amateur bands. The one exception is when it's incidental to an authorized retransmission of communications from a manned spacecraft.\n\nFor example, if NASA audio from an astronaut includes background music, a ham station relaying that feed with NASA's prior approval is allowed to carry it.",
"T1D05":"Hams may mention amateur equipment they're selling or trading, but not on a regular basis. Occasional swap announcements are fine.\n\nRunning a business on the air, or selling non-ham items, isn't allowed. The rule protects the noncommercial nature of the service.",
"T1D06":"Indecent and obscene language is prohibited on the amateur bands.\n\nThe FCC doesn't publish a list of banned words. Use the standard you'd follow speaking in public, since anyone, including kids, may be listening.",
"T1D07":"An auxiliary station sends one-way transmissions that support another station, such as linking a remote receiver site back to the main repeater transmitter, or carrying control signals.\n\nAuxiliary stations are the plumbing behind repeater systems. Remember auxiliary as one-way support links.",
"T1D08":"Amateur radio is noncommercial, so operators generally can't be paid for operating. One exception: a teacher may be paid when operating is part of classroom instruction at an educational institution.\n\nThat lets schools use ham radio in lessons without breaking the rules.",
"T1D09":"Amateurs may help broadcasters or news crews only when the information directly relates to the immediate safety of human life or protection of property, and only when no other means of communication is available.\n\nIt's an emergency exception, not a way to supply routine news.",
"T1D10":"The FCC defines broadcasting in the amateur service as transmissions intended for reception by the general public.\n\nThat's why broadcasting is prohibited: hams talk to other hams. Announcements aimed at hams, like a club net notice, aren't broadcasting.",
"T1D11":"Signals sent only to control a model craft, like an RC plane or boat, don't need on-air identification. Instead, the transmitter must carry a label showing the call sign and the licensee's name and address.\n\nThe other choices are traps: brief adjustments, unmodulated carriers and very low power all still need your call sign. Space stations and telecommand stations are also exempt, but they aren't offered here.",
"T1D12":"Test transmissions still must be identified with your call sign, at least every 10 minutes and at the end.\n\nIf you're tuning an antenna or checking a radio on the air, give your call sign. Better still, use a dummy load when you can.",
"T1E01":"Every amateur station that transmits must have a control operator, a licensed ham responsible for it. The answer is never.\n\nEven automatic stations, like repeaters, have a control operator who sets up the system and answers for its operation.",
"T1E02":"When using an amateur satellite, the control operator can be any ham whose license allows transmitting on the satellite's uplink frequency.\n\nMost satellite uplinks are on 2 meters or 70 cm, where Technicians have full privileges. So yes, Technicians can work satellites.",
"T1E03":"The station licensee, the person whose call sign the station uses, decides who the control operator is.\n\nYou can operate a friend's station with their permission. They designate you as control operator, and you follow your own license limits.",
"T1E04":"Transmitting privileges follow the control operator's license class, not the station owner's.\n\nA Technician running an Extra's station still has only Technician privileges. An Extra using a Technician's station may use full Extra privileges, but must then identify with the station's call followed by their own, such as KF1XYZ/K1AB.",
"T1E05":"The control point is where the control operator actually controls the station: the radio's front panel, a remote computer or a phone app.\n\nWith remote operation, the control point can be far from the transmitter itself. Control point means where the controlling happens.",
"T1E06":"Outside an emergency, a Technician may never be the control operator in a band segment reserved for Amateur Extra licensees.\n\nPrivileges follow the control operator's license, so a Technician can't borrow an Extra's privileges even at the Extra's station.",
"T1E07":"When the control operator isn't the station licensee, both are responsible for the station operating properly.\n\nThe owner can't hand off all accountability, and the guest operator can't claim it was someone else's radio. Shared responsibility.",
"T1E08":"Automatic control means the station operates without a control operator at a control point, with safeguards built in. A repeater is the classic example.\n\nIt listens on one frequency and retransmits on another by itself, around the clock, while its control operator is responsible but not watching every transmission.",
"T1E09":"Any amateur station may be operated by remote control, as long as the control operator follows the rules from the remote control point.\n\nRemote operation is common today, letting hams run home stations from work or while traveling.",
"T1E10":"Remote control means the control operator is at a control point away from the station, for example operating your home radio over the internet.\n\nDon't confuse it with telecommand, which is controlling a model aircraft or a space station by radio.",
"T1E11":"A control operator is an amateur designated by the station licensee to be responsible for the station's transmissions and FCC rule compliance.\n\nOften the licensee is the control operator of their own station, but they can name someone else. The definition stresses responsibility and designation.",
"T1F01":"An FCC representative may inspect your station and its records at any time upon request. No appointment, warrant or advance notice is needed.\n\nAgreeing to that is a condition of holding the license. In practice it's rare, but the rule is clear.",
"T1F02":"Tactical call signs like \"Race Headquarters\" help clarity during events, but they don't replace your FCC call.\n\nYou must still give your assigned call sign at least every 10 minutes during the conversation and at the end. The same 10-minute rule applies everywhere.",
"T1F03":"Give your call sign at least every 10 minutes during a contact and when you finish. Identifying at the start is polite but not required.\n\nA timer helps on long contacts. Remember the pattern: every 10 minutes and at the end.",
"T1F04":"When identifying by voice, you must use English. The FCC needs to recognize call signs anywhere.\n\nYou can carry on the rest of the conversation in any language, but the call sign itself goes out in English.",
"T1F05":"On phone, you may identify either by voice or by sending your call sign in CW (Morse code).\n\nMany repeaters identify themselves in CW automatically. Voice-only isn't required, which is why answer D is wrong: a CW ID counts too.",
"T1F06":"Indicators like /W3 or /M (mobile) can be added to a call sign. When speaking, you may say stroke, slant or slash, as in \"KL7CC stroke W3.\"\n\nAll are acceptable, so the answer is all of the choices.",
"T1F07":"A person without a license may talk to a foreign station through your station only if the foreign country has a third-party agreement with the United States.\n\nWithout that agreement, international messages for an unlicensed person aren't allowed. Your station's control operator must be present.",
"T1F08":"Third-party communication is a message from one control operator to another on behalf of someone else, a third party, who usually isn't a ham.\n\nPassing a message for a non-ham friend, or letting them speak through your station, is third-party traffic.",
"T1F09":"A repeater receives a signal on one frequency and simultaneously retransmits it on another, usually from a high site with more power, extending the range of handhelds and mobiles.\n\nSimultaneous retransmission on a different channel is the definition of a repeater.",
"T1F10":"If a repeater accidentally retransmits a rule-breaking message, the control operator of the originating station is responsible, not the repeater owner or its control operator.\n\nThe person who started the transmission is accountable for its content.",
"T1F11":"A club station license requires at least four members.\n\nThe trustee can hold any license class, and the club doesn't need to register with the ARRL. Four people make a club.",
"T2A01":"A repeater listens on one frequency (its input) and transmits on another (its output). On 2 meters, the two are usually 600 kHz apart.\n\nWhether you add or subtract the 600 kHz depends on the repeater, which is why it's \"plus or minus.\" Most radios set the offset automatically from the band plan. Pair it: 2 meters, 600 kHz; 70 cm, 5 MHz.",
"T2A02":"146.520 MHz is the national FM simplex calling frequency on 2 meters. Simplex means direct radio-to-radio contact, with no repeater.\n\nCall there, make contact, then move to another simplex frequency to chat so the calling channel stays open. It's also a good place to listen when traveling.",
"T2A03":"On the 70-centimeter band (420 to 450 MHz), repeaters usually use a 5 MHz offset between input and output.\n\nHigher bands use bigger offsets because they're wider and the transmitter and receiver need more separation to coexist. Remember: 2 meters, 600 kHz; 70 cm, 5 MHz.",
"T2A04":"On a repeater, if you're looking for a specific station, say their call sign, then give yours, like \"W1ABC, this is KF1XYZ.\"\n\nCalling CQ on a repeater isn't customary, and \"break break\" is reserved for urgent situations. Their call first, then yours.",
"T2A05":"To answer a CQ, say the calling station's call sign followed by your own. That tells them you're calling them, and who you are.\n\nThe same order applies everywhere: the station you're calling first, then your identification.",
"T2A06":"Off the repeaters, on simplex or HF, a general call is made with \"CQ.\" Say CQ a few times, then \"this is\" and your call sign, then listen.\n\nIf no one answers, repeat. Keep calls short so stations tuning by can catch you.",
"T2A07":"A repeater's offset is the difference between the frequency it listens on and the frequency it transmits on.\n\nYour radio listens on the output and transmits on the input, so it must be set to the right offset to reach the repeater.",
"T2A08":"CQ is a general call meaning \"calling any station.\" It invites anyone who hears it to answer.\n\nIt dates back to telegraph days and is used in voice, CW and digital modes alike.",
"T2A09":"On a repeater, the custom is to say your call sign followed by \"listening\" or \"monitoring.\" That tells others you're available for a chat.\n\nCQ is common on HF and simplex, but on repeaters \"listening\" is the norm.",
"T2A10":"A band plan is a voluntary guideline that says which modes or activities go where within a band, such as CW at the bottom, then SSB, then FM and repeaters.\n\nIt adds detail on top of the FCC's rules but isn't itself law. Following it keeps operators from interfering with each other. Voluntary but respected.",
"T2A11":"Simplex means transmitting and receiving on the same frequency, talking directly radio to radio.\n\nDuplex, used by repeaters, means separate transmit and receive frequencies. Simplex: same frequency, no repeater.",
"T2B01":"The reverse function swaps your radio's transmit and receive frequencies so you hear the repeater's input, where the other station is transmitting.\n\nIf you can hear them directly, you can move to a simplex frequency and leave the repeater free for others.",
"T2B02":"CTCSS, Continuous Tone-Coded Squelch System, sends a low tone below the range of hearing along with your voice. A repeater set to that tone opens only when it hears it.\n\nThis keeps the repeater from responding to interference or distant users. Many radios call it \"tone\" or \"PL.\"",
"T2B03":"A linked repeater network connects several repeaters so that whatever one receives, all of them transmit. You can talk through one repeater and be heard across a wide region.\n\nLinks may use radio or the internet.",
"T2B04":"If you can hear a repeater but can't get into it, several settings could be wrong: the offset, a missing CTCSS tone or a wrong DCS code.\n\nAny of these keeps the repeater from accepting your signal, so the answer is all of the choices. Check the repeater's listing for its exact settings.",
"T2B05":"FM signals vary their frequency with your voice. If you talk too loudly or too close to the mic, you overdeviate: your signal swings wider than the receiving radio's filter can pass.\n\nThe result is distorted audio that cuts out on voice peaks. Speak normally, with the mic a few inches away.",
"T2B06":"DTMF, Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency, sends two audio tones at once for each key. It's the same system as a phone keypad.\n\nHams use DTMF to send commands to repeaters, such as turning on links or reading out status.",
"T2B07":"On DMR (Digital Mobile Radio) repeaters, a talkgroup is a channel within the system. You join one by programming its ID number into your radio's channel settings.\n\nThere's no sign-up for a talkgroup: set the right ID and you're in. Using DMR at all does require your own DMR ID number, a free one-time online registration, but that's a separate step.",
"T2B08":"No one owns a frequency. When two stations interfere with each other, they should talk it out and decide who stays and who moves.\n\nCourtesy and cooperation are expected. Nets and emergencies get extra consideration.",
"T2B09":"Band plans set aside simplex channels so nearby stations can talk directly without tying up a repeater.\n\nRepeaters serve many users, so if you're close enough to reach each other directly, simplex is the courteous choice.",
"T2B10":"Q signals are three-letter codes starting with Q. QRM means man-made interference from other stations. QRN means natural noise, like static from lightning.\n\nMemory hook: QRM, M for man-made; QRN, N for natural.",
"T2B11":"QSY means \"change frequency\" or \"I'm changing frequency.\" Hams say \"let's QSY to 146.55\" when moving a conversation off a calling channel.\n\nOther common ones: QTH is location, QSL is acknowledgment.",
"T2B12":"On DMR repeaters, the color code is an access code your radio must transmit to use a particular repeater. It works like a CTCSS tone does on an FM repeater.\n\nWithout the right color code, the repeater ignores your signal.",
"T2B13":"Squelch mutes the receiver's audio when no signal is present, cutting the constant hiss you'd otherwise hear.\n\nWhen a signal arrives, squelch opens and you hear the station. Set it just past where the noise stops.",
"T2B14":"On DMR, a talkgroup is an identifier that sorts radio traffic. Your radio plays only the groups you've chosen, so you aren't bothered by everything else on the system.\n\nTalkgroups can be local, regional or worldwide.",
"T2C01":"FCC rules always apply to amateur stations. There's no exemption for emergencies, public service events, RACES or ARES.\n\nDuring a real emergency, some flexibility exists, like operating outside your privileges to protect life, but that comes from the rules themselves.",
"T2C02":"The Net Control Station runs a net. It calls the net to order, takes check-ins and directs which station transmits when.\n\nThis keeps busy nets orderly, especially during emergencies when many stations need to pass information.",
"T2C03":"When a word is unusual or easily misheard, spell it with a standard phonetic alphabet: Alpha, Bravo, Charlie and so on.\n\nThat makes names, addresses and technical terms come through correctly, even with noise.",
"T2C04":"RACES, Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service, is an FCC-authorized service under Part 97 for civil defense communications. It's activated during emergencies by government agencies.\n\nUnlike ARES, members must be certified by a civil defense organization.",
"T2C05":"In net operation, traffic means formal messages exchanged between stations, often radiograms with a standard format.\n\nTraffic nets pass messages across the country, and during emergencies they relay health and welfare information.",
"T2C06":"ARES, the Amateur Radio Emergency Service, is a group of licensed hams who voluntarily register their skills and equipment for public service communications.\n\nIt's organized by the ARRL and works with local agencies. No government certification is required, unlike RACES.",
"T2C07":"In a directed net, you transmit only when net control tells you to, unless you're reporting an emergency.\n\nThis keeps the net efficient and avoids people talking over each other.",
"T2C08":"Winlink is a system that sends email over amateur radio. Addresses are built from call signs, like KF1XYZ@winlink.org.\n\nIt's a key emergency tool when internet service is down.",
"T2C09":"Operators may go outside their license privileges only in emergencies involving immediate safety of human life or protection of property.\n\nIf someone's life is at stake and no other way exists, any frequency or mode may be used.",
"T2C10":"A formal message, or radiogram, has a preamble at the top. It contains tracking information: message number, precedence, the station of origin, the check, and the time and date.\n\nIt helps relay stations handle and trace the message.",
"T2C11":"In a radiogram's preamble, the \"check\" is the number of words in the message text.\n\nThe receiving station counts the words and compares. If they don't match, something was lost or added.",
"T2C12":"RACES requires certification by a civil defense agency because it operates under government emergency management.\n\nARES, MARS (the military affiliate system) and SKYWARN (storm spotters) don't require civil defense certification.",
"T3A01":"At VHF, signals reach you by several routes at once: directly, and bounced off buildings, hills and vehicles. Those copies arrive slightly out of step.\n\nWhere they line up, they reinforce; where they're opposite, they cancel. Moving the antenna a few feet changes the mix, so strength can jump or vanish. That's multipath.",
"T3A02":"Leaves and branches are full of water, which absorbs energy at UHF and microwave frequencies. The shorter the wavelength, the worse it gets.\n\nStrong signals may push through, but weak ones get lost. Microwave links are often planned around tree lines for this reason.",
"T3A03":"For long-distance weak-signal work on VHF and UHF, using CW or SSB, operators use horizontally polarized antennas, such as horizontal Yagis.\n\nFM and repeater operation use vertical polarization, since vertical antennas are easy on cars and handhelds. Mixing the two costs signal strength.",
"T3A04":"Cross-polarization means one antenna is vertical and the other horizontal. Over a direct line-of-sight path, the receiving antenna picks up only a small fraction of the signal.\n\nThe loss can be 20 dB or more, meaning a hundredth of the power or less gets through. Match polarization: vertical to vertical, horizontal to horizontal.",
"T3A05":"Radio waves reflect off buildings, mountains and other large surfaces. If the direct path to a repeater is blocked, aim your directional antenna to bounce the signal off something toward it.\n\nTry different directions and listen for the best signal. It's like using a mirror to see around a corner.",
"T3A06":"Picket fencing is rapid flutter heard on mobile VHF and UHF signals. As the car moves, multipath reflections change quickly, so the signal strength pulses.\n\nThe name comes from the sound of a stick dragged along a picket fence.",
"T3A07":"At microwave frequencies, rain, snow and fog absorb and scatter radio energy. The drops are a significant size compared with the very short wavelengths.\n\nHeavy rain can shorten a microwave link's range noticeably. Lower bands are barely affected.",
"T3A08":"Signals bouncing off the ionosphere can take several paths that shift as the ionosphere changes. The copies combine at random, sometimes adding, sometimes canceling.\n\nThe result is irregular fading, where a station swells and fades over seconds or minutes.",
"T3A09":"When a signal travels through the ionosphere, its polarization twists, coming back elliptically polarized, a mix of vertical and horizontal.\n\nSo on HF skywave contacts, polarization barely matters. A vertical antenna can talk to a horizontal one without the big loss you'd see at VHF.",
"T3A10":"Multipath delivers several copies of a signal at slightly different times. For data, that blurs the timing of each bit or symbol.\n\nThe receiver struggles to tell one bit from the next, so error rates go up. Many digital modes include error correction to cope.",
"T3A11":"The ionosphere is a layer of the upper atmosphere ionized by solar radiation, roughly 50 to 600 km up. It can bend HF radio waves back to Earth.\n\nThat's what makes long-distance HF contacts possible. The troposphere (where weather happens) and the stratosphere are lower and not ionized enough to do this, and \"electrosphere\" isn't a standard atmospheric layer.",
"T3A12":"Fog and rain have little effect on 10 and 6 meters. The waves are long compared with water droplets, so they pass right through.\n\nWeather absorption becomes a real factor only at microwave frequencies, several gigahertz and up.",
"T3B01":"A radio wave is made of an electric field and a magnetic field. They're at right angles to each other, and both are at right angles to the direction the wave travels.\n\nPicture three perpendicular arrows: electric up, magnetic sideways, travel forward.",
"T3B02":"Polarization is defined by the orientation of the electric field. A vertical antenna produces a vertical electric field, so its wave is vertically polarized.\n\nMatch the antenna to the polarization for the strongest signal.",
"T3B03":"A radio wave has two parts: an electric field and a magnetic field. They travel together and constantly regenerate each other.\n\nThat's why it's called an electromagnetic wave.",
"T3B04":"Radio waves are electromagnetic waves, just like light. In free space they travel at the speed of light.\n\nThat's about 300,000,000 meters per second, the source of the 300 in the wavelength formula.",
"T3B05":"Radio waves all travel at the same speed. Higher frequency means more cycles per second, so each cycle must be shorter.\n\nWavelength and frequency are inversely related: double the frequency, halve the wavelength.",
"T3B06":"Wavelength in meters equals 300 divided by frequency in MHz. The 300 comes from the speed of light, 300 million meters per second.\n\nExample: 146 MHz gives 300 / 146, about 2 meters. That's why it's called the 2-meter band.",
"T3B07":"Amateur bands are often named by their approximate wavelength in meters: 2 meters, 6 meters, 70 centimeters and so on.\n\nUse 300 / MHz to connect the names to frequencies.",
"T3B08":"VHF, Very High Frequency, covers 30 to 300 MHz. It includes 6 meters, 2 meters and the 1.25-meter band.\n\nThe ranges go in steps of ten: HF 3 to 30, VHF 30 to 300, UHF 300 to 3,000 MHz.",
"T3B09":"UHF, Ultra High Frequency, covers 300 to 3,000 MHz. It includes the 70-centimeter band (420 to 450 MHz) and 33 and 23 centimeters.\n\nPattern: each range is ten times the one below.",
"T3B10":"HF, High Frequency, runs 3 to 30 MHz. It holds many ham bands; Technicians have limited privileges on four of them: 80, 40, 15 and 10 meters.\n\nHF is where the ionosphere enables worldwide contacts.",
"T3B11":"Radio waves travel at the speed of light, about 300,000,000 meters per second.\n\nThat number gives the 300 in the wavelength formula: wavelength equals 300 divided by frequency in MHz.",
"T3B12":"All radio frequencies travel at the same speed in free space: the speed of light.\n\nFrequency changes the wavelength, not the speed.",
"T3C01":"UHF signals usually pass straight through the ionosphere instead of bending back to Earth. Without a reflector, they travel in roughly straight lines.\n\nOnce past the radio horizon, the curve of the Earth blocks them, so UHF simplex is mostly local.",
"T3C02":"On HF, the ionosphere commonly bends signals back to Earth, enabling contacts across continents.\n\nOn VHF and above, that happens far less often, so those bands are mostly line of sight, with occasional special propagation.\n\nThe other choices are backward: HF antennas are larger (longer wavelengths), HF signals are narrower, and HF has more static, not less.",
"T3C03":"Aurora creates a reflecting curtain in the northern sky. VHF signals scattered off it come back distorted, with a raspy, buzzing sound.\n\nVoice can be hard to understand, so CW is often preferred during auroral openings.",
"T3C04":"Sporadic E forms patches of dense ionization in the E region of the ionosphere, usually in early summer. It can reflect 10, 6 and sometimes 2 meters.\n\nOpenings come suddenly and bring strong signals from hundreds to over a thousand miles away.",
"T3C05":"Knife-edge diffraction occurs when radio waves bend around a sharp edge, such as a ridge or building top.\n\nSome of the signal spills over into the area behind the obstruction, allowing contact where line of sight is blocked.",
"T3C06":"The troposphere is the lowest layer of the atmosphere, where weather happens. Tropospheric ducting traps VHF and UHF signals in a layer of it, usually under a temperature inversion, guiding them far beyond the normal horizon.\n\nRanges of around 300 miles are common, and sometimes much more: ducts over the Pacific have carried signals from California to Hawaii.",
"T3C07":"Meteors leave short-lived trails of ionized gas that can reflect radio signals. Lower frequencies reflect off these thin trails best, and 6 meters is the lowest-frequency band among the choices, so it works best.\n\nContacts last seconds, so operators use special digital modes. Meteor showers bring the best activity.",
"T3C08":"Normally air gets colder with height. In a temperature inversion, a layer of warm air sits above cooler air.\n\nThe boundary bends VHF and UHF signals and can trap them in a duct, carrying them far beyond the horizon.",
"T3C09":"Ten-meter F region propagation needs strong solar ionization, so it's best during daylight in high sunspot years.\n\nOpenings run from dawn to shortly after sunset. At night the ionosphere weakens, and 10 meters usually goes quiet.",
"T3C10":"At the peak of the sunspot cycle, the F region is ionized enough to return 10 and even 6 meters, making worldwide contacts possible.\n\nThe cycle lasts about 11 years.",
"T3C11":"The atmosphere bends VHF and UHF waves slightly back toward the Earth, so they follow its curve a little.\n\nAs a result, the radio horizon is roughly 15 percent farther than the visual horizon.",
"T4A01":"Mobile radios are designed for a car's electrical system, which runs about 13.8 volts with the engine on. A 50-watt transmitter isn't perfectly efficient, so it draws roughly 10 to 12 amps.\n\nP = I × E: 13.8 V × 12 A is about 165 watts of input, enough to cover 50 watts of RF plus losses. Choose a supply rated for at least 12 amps; 4 amps is far too little. The 24-volt choices are wrong because mobile radios are built for a car's 12-volt system.",
"T4A02":"An SWR meter measures how well your antenna system is matched. Meters are designed for particular frequency ranges and power levels.\n\nAn HF meter may be inaccurate at 440 MHz, and a low-power meter can be damaged by a big amplifier. Match the meter to the frequency and power you'll use.",
"T4A03":"When transmitting, a radio may draw 10 amps or more. Long or thin wires have more resistance, so the voltage drops before reaching the radio.\n\nE = I × R: even 0.1 ohm at 12 amps loses 1.2 volts. Short, heavy-gauge wires keep the radio at full voltage.",
"T4A04":"For FT8, the computer does the work. The radio's audio output goes to the computer's audio input so the software can decode signals. The computer's audio output goes to the radio's audio input to transmit.\n\nIt's a loop: radio out to computer in, computer out to radio in.",
"T4A05":"An RF power meter measures the energy going to the antenna, so it goes in the feed line between the transmitter and the antenna.\n\nMany meters also measure reflected power, which gives SWR.",
"T4A06":"A computer-radio interface needs three things: receive audio from radio to computer, transmit audio from computer to radio, and a keying signal to switch the radio into transmit.\n\nMany modern radios handle all three over one USB cable.",
"T4A07":"To decode digital signals, the computer needs the radio's received audio. So the radio's speaker output connects to the computer's line in.\n\nThe opposite path, computer out to radio mic or data input, carries transmit audio.",
"T4A08":"At radio frequencies, current flows mostly on the surface of a conductor (the skin effect). A flat copper strap has much more surface area than a round wire.\n\nThat gives lower impedance at RF, so bonding works better. Wide and flat beats round and thin.",
"T4A09":"A battery's ampere-hour (Ah) rating says how much current it can supply over time. Divide that by your equipment's average current draw to get run time in hours.\n\nExample: a 20 Ah battery with a radio averaging 4 A lasts about five hours.",
"T4A10":"A hotspot is a small, low-power device that connects nearby digital radios, like DMR or D-STAR handhelds, to an internet-based digital voice or data network.\n\nIt acts as a personal gateway when no repeater is in range.",
"T4A11":"In a vehicle, connect the radio's negative lead at the battery's chassis ground point. That avoids noise and voltage problems from poor body grounds.\n\nThe positive lead goes to the battery too, with fuses close to the battery.",
"T4A12":"An electronic keyer helps you send Morse code by hand. Using a paddle, it creates evenly timed dots and dashes automatically.\n\nYou control the rhythm, and the keyer keeps each element precise.",
"T4B01":"On SSB, too much microphone gain overdrives the transmitter. The audio becomes distorted, and the signal can spread out and interfere with nearby frequencies.\n\nSet mic gain so the ALC (automatic level control) meter stays within the range the manual recommends.",
"T4B02":"Modern transceivers let you set the frequency with the keypad, typing it directly, or by turning the VFO (variable frequency oscillator) knob.\n\nThe other choices don't set frequency: CTCSS and DTMF are audio tones used with repeaters, and AFC only fine-tunes onto a signal you're already near.",
"T4B03":"Squelch mutes the receiver when no signal is present. A very weak signal may not be strong enough to open it.\n\nSet the squelch so the audio stays on all the time, accepting the noise, and you'll hear even weak signals.",
"T4B04":"An FM receiver works best when tuned exactly to the signal. If you're slightly off frequency, the audio becomes distorted.\n\nThat's different from SSB, where being off frequency makes the voice sound higher or lower in pitch. On FM the pitch doesn't shift; the audio just gets harsh and distorted.",
"T4B05":"Scanning steps the radio through a set of frequencies or channels and pauses when it finds activity.\n\nIt's a handy way to find active repeaters or simplex conversations.",
"T4B06":"On SSB, a signal slightly off frequency makes the voice sound too high or too low. RIT, Receiver Incremental Tuning (also called the clarifier), shifts only your receive frequency.\n\nYou can fix the voice without moving your transmit frequency.",
"T4B07":"A DMR code plug is the configuration file loaded into a DMR radio. It contains repeater frequencies, color codes, talkgroups and contacts.\n\nYou usually create it with programming software and transfer it by cable.",
"T4B08":"Different modes use different bandwidths: CW needs a few hundred hertz, SSB about 2.4 kHz, and FM much more.\n\nA filter that matches the mode lets the signal through while blocking noise and interference outside it.",
"T4B09":"On DMR, groups are organized as talkgroups, each with an identification number. Select a group by entering its ID in your radio's channel settings.\n\nThen you hear and talk only to that group.",
"T4B10":"An SSB voice signal is about 2.4 to 3 kHz wide. A 2,400 Hz filter lets the voice through while cutting extra noise.\n\nA narrower filter would clip the voice, and a wider one would add noise. 2,400 Hz is the sweet spot.",
"T4B11":"D-STAR identifies every transmission digitally with your call sign, so the radio must have it programmed in before you transmit.\n\nThe network uses it for routing and identification.",
"T5A01":"Current is the flow of electric charge, and its unit is the ampere, or amp. One amp is a lot of electrons passing a point every second.\n\nUnits to keep straight: amps for current, volts for voltage, ohms for resistance, watts for power. Amps are what an ammeter measures in series with a circuit.",
"T5A02":"Power is the rate of using or producing energy, measured in watts. A 100-watt transmitter turns 100 joules of energy into RF every second.\n\nWatt-hours measure total energy used over time, like on an electric bill. Watts are the rate; watt-hours are the amount.",
"T5A03":"Current is the flow of electrons through a circuit. Voltage is the push that causes the flow, and resistance is what limits it.\n\nThe plumbing picture helps: voltage is water pressure, current is the water flowing, resistance is a narrow pipe.",
"T5A04":"Alternating current swings back and forth. One full swing, forward and back, is a cycle. The number of cycles per second is the frequency.\n\nHousehold power in the US is 60 cycles per second, 60 Hz. Radio signals do the same thing millions of times a second.",
"T5A05":"Electrons flow because of a difference in voltage, also called electromotive force or potential difference, between two points.\n\nLike water flowing from high pressure to low, electrons move from one end of a battery through the circuit to the other. No voltage difference, no current.",
"T5A06":"Frequency is measured in hertz (Hz). One hertz is one cycle per second.\n\nRadio uses multiples: kilohertz (thousands), megahertz (millions), gigahertz (billions). The 2-meter band is around 146 MHz, 146 million cycles per second.",
"T5A07":"In metals, outer electrons aren't tightly bound to atoms. They drift freely and can move when a voltage is applied.\n\nThat sea of free electrons makes copper, silver and aluminum excellent conductors.",
"T5A08":"Glass holds its electrons tightly, so current can't flow through it easily. That makes it a good insulator.\n\nSea water, steel and graphite all have mobile charges and conduct. Glass and ceramic insulators are common on antennas and power lines.",
"T5A09":"Alternating current reverses direction regularly, flowing one way, then the other. It swings between positive and negative many times a second.\n\nCurrent that swings between positive and zero (or negative and zero) never reverses, so it's pulsing direct current, not AC. That rules out the other choices and \"All these.\" Direct current, like from a battery, flows in one direction only.",
"T5A10":"Power is the rate at which electrical energy is used or produced, measured in watts.\n\nEnergy is the total amount used; power is how fast you use it. Think of a car trip: power is like speed, energy is like the distance covered.",
"T5A11":"Resistance opposes the flow of any current: direct current (DC), alternating current (AC) and radio-frequency current (RF).\n\nThat's why all of the choices are correct. Coils and capacitors add more opposition to AC and RF, called reactance.",
"T5B01":"Milli means one-thousandth. There are 1,000 milliamperes in one ampere.\n\nGoing from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply: 1.5 A × 1,000 = 1,500 mA. Smaller units, bigger numbers.",
"T5B02":"Kilo means thousand. To change hertz to kilohertz, divide by 1,000.\n\n1,500,000 Hz ÷ 1,000 = 1,500 kHz. Going to a bigger unit, the number gets smaller. It's also 1.5 MHz, but that answer isn't offered.",
"T5B03":"Kilo means one thousand. One kilovolt is 1,000 volts.\n\nSame prefix as kilometer (1,000 meters) and kilogram (1,000 grams).",
"T5B04":"Micro means one-millionth. One microvolt is 0.000001 volt.\n\nThe trap answer, one-thousandth, is milli. Order from small to large: pico, nano, micro, milli, base unit, kilo, mega, giga.",
"T5B05":"Milli means one-thousandth, so 1,000 milliwatts make one watt.\n\n500 mW ÷ 1,000 = 0.5 W, half a watt. Converting to the bigger unit, divide.",
"T5B06":"There are 1,000 milliamperes in one ampere.\n\n3,000 mA ÷ 1,000 = 3 A. Moving to the bigger unit, divide by 1,000: slide the decimal three places left.",
"T5B07":"There are 1,000 kilohertz in a megahertz. Going to the smaller unit, multiply.\n\n3.525 MHz × 1,000 = 3,525 kHz. That frequency is in the 80-meter band.",
"T5B08":"Pico means one-trillionth; micro means one-millionth. A microfarad is a million times bigger than a picofarad.\n\nPico to nano is one step of 1,000, nano to micro is another: 1,000 × 1,000 = 1,000,000. So 1,000,000 pF ÷ 1,000,000 = 1 µF.",
"T5B09":"Decibels measure ratios on a log scale. Doubling power is about 3 dB.\n\n5 W to 10 W doubles, so +3 dB. The same is true from 50 to 100 W or 500 to 1,000 W.",
"T5B10":"Halving power is −3 dB. From 12 W to 6 W is one halving, and 6 W to 3 W is another.\n\nTwo halvings make −6 dB. A quarter of the power is −6 dB.",
"T5B11":"Ten times the power is +10 dB. From 20 W to 200 W is a tenfold increase.\n\nKey dB values: 2x = 3 dB, 4x = 6 dB, 10x = 10 dB.",
"T5B12":"To change kilohertz to megahertz, divide by 1,000.\n\n28,400 kHz ÷ 1,000 = 28.400 MHz, in the 10-meter band, inside the Technician phone segment.",
"T5B13":"Giga means billion; mega means million. There are 1,000 MHz in 1 GHz.\n\n2,425 MHz ÷ 1,000 = 2.425 GHz, near Wi-Fi and microwave oven frequencies.",
"T5C01":"Capacitance is the ability to store energy in an electric field, between conductive plates separated by an insulator.\n\nIts partner, inductance, stores energy in a magnetic field. Electric field, capacitance; magnetic field, inductance.",
"T5C02":"Capacitance is measured in farads, named after Michael Faraday.\n\nA farad is huge, so circuits use microfarads (µF) and picofarads (pF).",
"T5C03":"Inductance is the ability to store energy in a magnetic field, usually in a coil of wire.\n\nCurrent through the coil builds up a magnetic field. When the current changes, the field pushes back, resisting the change.",
"T5C04":"Inductance is measured in henries, named after Joseph Henry.\n\nRadio circuits use millihenries (mH) and microhenries (µH).",
"T5C05":"Impedance is total opposition to AC current, combining resistance and reactance. Like resistance, it's measured in ohms.\n\nThat's why antennas and coax are rated in ohms, like 50 ohms.",
"T5C06":"Kilohertz is written kHz: lowercase k for kilo, capital H for Hertz, lowercase z.\n\nCapital K isn't standard for kilo. The capital H honors Heinrich Hertz.",
"T5C07":"Megahertz is MHz: capital M for mega, capital H for Hertz, lowercase z.\n\nCase matters: lowercase m means milli, one-thousandth. MHz (a million hertz) is a billion times bigger than mHz (a thousandth of a hertz).",
"T5C08":"Power equals current times voltage: P = I × E. In ham notation, E stands for voltage (electromotive force) and I for current.\n\nMemory aid: PIE. P = I × E.",
"T5C09":"P = I × E. 10 amps × 13.8 volts = 138 watts.\n\n13.8 volts is the typical voltage of a car's electrical system, so this is the power for a mobile radio drawing 10 amps.",
"T5C10":"P = I × E. 2.5 amps × 12 volts = 30 watts.\n\nCheck: 12 × 2 = 24, plus 12 × 0.5 = 6, total 30.",
"T5C11":"Rearrange P = I × E to find current: I = P ÷ E.\n\n120 watts ÷ 12 volts = 10 amps. Memory aid: picture a triangle with P on top and I and E side by side below. Cover I, and P sits over E, so divide.",
"T5C12":"Impedance is total opposition to AC current, combining resistance with reactance from coils and capacitors.\n\nIt's measured in ohms. Matching impedance between radio, coax and antenna transfers power efficiently.",
"T5D01":"Ohm's law: current equals voltage divided by resistance, I = E ÷ R.\n\nMore voltage means more current; more resistance means less. Memory aid: picture a triangle with E on top and I and R side by side below. Cover I, and E sits over R, so divide.",
"T5D02":"Ohm's law: voltage equals current times resistance, E = I × R.\n\nIn the triangle, cover E and I sits beside R, so multiply.",
"T5D03":"Ohm's law: resistance equals voltage divided by current, R = E ÷ I.\n\nIn the triangle, cover R and E sits over I, so divide.",
"T5D04":"R = E ÷ I = 90 volts ÷ 3 amps = 30 ohms.\n\nCheck: 3 amps × 30 ohms = 90 volts. It works.",
"T5D05":"R = E ÷ I = 12 volts ÷ 1.5 amps = 8 ohms.\n\nCheck: 1.5 × 8 = 12. Tip: to divide by 1.5, double both numbers first: 12 ÷ 1.5 is the same as 24 ÷ 3 = 8.",
"T5D06":"R = E ÷ I = 12 volts ÷ 4 amps = 3 ohms.\n\nCheck: 4 amps × 3 ohms = 12 volts.",
"T5D07":"I = E ÷ R = 120 volts ÷ 80 ohms = 1.5 amps.\n\nThe trap answer, 0.667, comes from dividing the wrong way. Voltage goes on top.",
"T5D08":"I = E ÷ R = 200 volts ÷ 100 ohms = 2 amps.\n\nCheck: 2 × 100 = 200.",
"T5D09":"I = E ÷ R = 240 volts ÷ 24 ohms = 10 amps.\n\nCheck: 10 × 24 = 240.",
"T5D10":"E = I × R = 0.5 amp × 2 ohms = 1 volt.\n\nHalf an amp through two ohms makes one volt.",
"T5D11":"E = I × R = 1 amp × 10 ohms = 10 volts.\n\nWith one amp, the voltage equals the resistance.",
"T5D12":"E = I × R = 2 amps × 10 ohms = 20 volts.\n\nDouble the current, double the voltage.",
"T5D13":"In a series circuit, components connect end to end in one path. The same current flows through every part.\n\nVoltage divides among them, but current stays the same. Series: same current.",
"T5D14":"In a parallel circuit, every component connects across the same two points, so each sees the same voltage.\n\nCurrent divides among the branches. Parallel: same voltage.",
"T6A01":"A resistor is built to oppose current. It's often a small cylinder with colored bands showing its value in ohms.\n\nResistors limit current, divide voltage and set operating levels. An inductor opposes changes in current, but in a steady DC circuit it acts almost like a plain wire.",
"T6A02":"A potentiometer is a resistor with a sliding contact controlled by a knob. Turning it changes the resistance between the slider and the ends.\n\nThat's how a volume control works: the knob sets how much of the audio signal passes on to the amplifier and speaker.",
"T6A03":"A potentiometer is a variable resistor, so it controls resistance. Turning the knob moves a contact along a resistive track.\n\nIts name comes from \"potential,\" another word for voltage: it can tap off any fraction of the voltage across it, but what you are actually adjusting is resistance.",
"T6A04":"A capacitor stores energy in an electric field between two conductive plates separated by an insulator.\n\nInductors store energy in a magnetic field. Hook: Capacitor, eleCtric; inductor, magnetic.",
"T6A05":"A capacitor is two conductive surfaces separated by an insulator called a dielectric.\n\nCharge builds up on the plates, creating an electric field across the insulator. Air, ceramic, plastic and paper are common dielectrics.",
"T6A06":"An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field. Current through a coil of wire creates the field.\n\nWhen current changes, the field resists the change. That's what makes inductors useful in filters and tuned circuits.",
"T6A07":"An inductor is usually a coil of wire, sometimes wound on a core of iron or ferrite to increase its inductance.\n\nThe schematic symbol even looks like a coil: a series of loops.",
"T6A08":"Switches are named by poles and throws. Poles are how many circuits it controls; throws are how many positions each pole can connect to.\n\nSPDT, single-pole double-throw, connects one circuit to either of two others, like each of the two switches that control one stairway light from the top and the bottom (in US stores these are called \"three-way\" switches).",
"T6A09":"Component 3 in figure T-2 is a single-pole single-throw (SPST) switch. It simply opens or closes one circuit, like an ordinary on/off switch.\n\nThe symbol is a single lever that touches one contact.",
"T6A10":"Nickel-metal hydride (NiMH), lithium-ion and lead-acid batteries can all be recharged many times.\n\nNiMH appears in AA packs, lithium-ion in phones and handhelds, lead-acid in cars and backup systems. All of the choices are correct.",
"T6A11":"Carbon-zinc batteries are the cheap disposable cells. Trying to recharge them doesn't work and can make them leak.\n\nNickel-cadmium (NiCd) batteries are rechargeable, so don't fall for that trap.",
"T6B01":"When a diode conducts, it drops a small voltage across itself, called the forward voltage drop. It varies by type.\n\nSilicon diodes drop about 0.7 volt. Germanium and Schottky diodes drop less, around 0.2 to 0.4 volt. So it's lower in some types than others.",
"T6B02":"A diode allows current to flow in only one direction, like a one-way valve.\n\nCurrent flows from anode to cathode. Diodes are used in rectifiers to change AC to DC.",
"T6B03":"A transistor can act as an electronic switch. A small control current or voltage turns a larger current on or off.\n\nTransistors can also amplify. Computers are built from billions of tiny transistor switches.",
"T6B04":"A bipolar junction transistor is built from three regions of semiconductor material: NPN or PNP.\n\nTriodes and pentodes are vacuum tubes, not semiconductors.",
"T6B05":"A field-effect transistor (FET) has three electrodes: gate, drain and source. The voltage on the gate controls current between drain and source.\n\nBipolar transistors have emitter, base and collector.",
"T6B06":"Diodes are usually marked with a stripe or band near the cathode end.\n\nThat matches the bar in the schematic symbol, where the triangle points toward the cathode.",
"T6B07":"An LED gives off light when forward current flows through it, from anode to cathode.\n\nReverse current doesn't flow, so the LED stays dark. LEDs are widely used as indicators.",
"T6B08":"FET stands for Field Effect Transistor. An electric field from the gate voltage controls current flow.\n\nFETs draw almost no input current, which makes them useful in sensitive circuits.",
"T6B09":"A diode's two electrodes are the anode and the cathode. Current flows from anode to cathode.\n\nSource and drain are FET terms, not diode terms.",
"T6B10":"Power gain means the output delivers more power than the input. Only an active device, like a transistor or vacuum tube, can do that by drawing energy from a power supply.\n\nA transformer can raise voltage but lowers current, so power out never exceeds power in.",
"T6B11":"Gain means an increase in signal: voltage, current or power. An amplifier can provide any of them.\n\nSo all of the choices are correct. Gain is often expressed in decibels.",
"T6B12":"A bipolar junction transistor (BJT) has three electrodes: emitter, base and collector.\n\nThe base controls current between the collector and emitter. A small base current controls a much larger collector current.",
"T6C01":"A schematic is an electrical diagram using standard symbols. It shows how components connect, not how they look physically.\n\nLearning schematic symbols lets you read and build circuits.",
"T6C02":"Component 1 in figure T-1 is a resistor, drawn as a zigzag line.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.",
"T6C03":"Component 2 in figure T-1 is a transistor, shown as a circle with three leads.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.",
"T6C04":"Component 3 in figure T-1 is a lamp, a bulb-shaped symbol.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.",
"T6C05":"Component 4 in figure T-1 is a battery, shown as alternating long and short lines.\n\nFigure T-1 key: 1 resistor, 2 transistor, 3 lamp, 4 battery.",
"T6C06":"Component 6 in figure T-2 is a capacitor: two plates, one straight and one curved, with a gap.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.",
"T6C07":"Component 8 in figure T-2 is a light-emitting diode: a diode symbol with arrows pointing outward to show light.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.",
"T6C08":"Component 9 in figure T-2 is a variable resistor: a zigzag with an arrow through or pointing into it.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.",
"T6C09":"Component 4 in figure T-2 is a transformer: two coils side by side with lines between them for the core.\n\nFigure T-2 key: 3 SPST switch, 4 transformer, 6 capacitor, 8 LED, 9 variable resistor.",
"T6C10":"Component 3 in figure T-3 is a variable inductor: a coil with an arrow, showing it can be adjusted.\n\nFigure T-3 key: 3 variable inductor, 4 antenna.",
"T6C11":"Component 4 in figure T-3 is an antenna: an upside-down triangle on a stem.\n\nFigure T-3 key: 3 variable inductor, 4 antenna.",
"T6C12":"A schematic accurately shows how components connect. It doesn't show physical size, location or wire length.\n\nIt's a wiring logic diagram, not a map.",
"T6D01":"A rectifier changes AC into varying DC. It uses diodes to let current flow in only one direction.\n\nThe output pulses, so filter capacitors smooth it out in a power supply.",
"T6D02":"A relay is a switch operated by electricity. A small current through a coil creates a magnetic field that moves the switch contacts, closing (or on some relays opening) a circuit.\n\nRelays let small control signals switch large currents, like amplifier power.",
"T6D03":"Shielded wire has a metal braid or foil around the inner conductors. The shield blocks unwanted signals from getting into the wire and keeps the wire's signals from leaking out.\n\nCoax and shielded audio cables use this principle.",
"T6D04":"A meter displays an electrical quantity, like voltage or current, as a numeric value or a needle reading.\n\nDigital multimeters show numbers directly.",
"T6D05":"A voltage regulator keeps a power supply's output voltage steady, even when the load or input changes.\n\nRadios need stable voltage to work properly.",
"T6D06":"A transformer changes AC voltage up or down using two coils. From 120 V household power, it can produce a lower AC voltage.\n\nThe output is still AC; a rectifier converts it to DC.",
"T6D07":"LEDs are commonly used as visual indicators, like power and transmit lights on radios.\n\nA Zener diode regulates voltage and doesn't light up.",
"T6D08":"An inductor combined with a capacitor forms a resonant circuit. Energy swings back and forth between the inductor's magnetic field and the capacitor's electric field.\n\nThat tuned circuit picks out one frequency, the basis of radio tuning.",
"T6D09":"An integrated circuit (IC) packs many transistors, diodes, resistors and other parts onto one chip.\n\nICs range from simple amplifiers to complex processors.",
"T6D10":"Component 2 in figure T-1 is a transistor. Its job is to control the flow of current.\n\nIn that circuit, it switches the lamp on and off. The lamp, component 3, is what gives off light.",
"T6D11":"A resonant or tuned circuit is an inductor and a capacitor together, either in series or in parallel.\n\nIt responds strongly to one frequency, which is how radios select stations.",
"T7A01":"Sensitivity is how weak a signal a receiver can detect. A sensitive receiver hears stations other radios miss.\n\nIts partner is selectivity, the ability to pick one signal out from others nearby. Sensitivity hears; selectivity separates.",
"T7A02":"A transceiver combines a transmitter and a receiver in one unit, sharing parts like the frequency control and power supply.\n\nNearly all modern ham radios are transceivers, from handhelds like the FT-65R to big HF base stations.",
"T7A03":"A mixer takes two signals and produces new frequencies at their sum and difference. That shifts a signal from one frequency to another.\n\nExample: mix 14 MHz with 9 MHz and you get 23 MHz and 5 MHz; filters keep the one you want. Receivers use mixers to move every station to one fixed frequency where good filtering is easy. Hook: a mixer mixes, making new frequencies. An oscillator only makes one.",
"T7A04":"Selectivity is a receiver's ability to separate the signal you want from others on nearby frequencies. Good filters give good selectivity.\n\nThe trap is sensitivity, which is about hearing weak signals. Hook: Selectivity Selects one signal from many. Sensitivity Senses that a signal is there at all.",
"T7A05":"An oscillator generates a steady signal at a specific frequency. Oscillators are at the heart of nearly every transmitter and receiver.\n\nThe VFO, variable frequency oscillator, is the one you tune with the dial.",
"T7A06":"A transverter shifts a transceiver's RF to another band. It converts on both receive and transmit.\n\nFor example, it can let a 10-meter radio operate on 2 meters or 222 MHz. Trans-verter, like trans-ceiver: it works both directions.",
"T7A07":"PTT means push to talk. The PTT line is normally held open; grounding it, by pressing the mic button or a computer interface, switches the radio from receive to transmit.\n\nDigital mode interfaces use this line to key the radio automatically.",
"T7A08":"Modulation is the process of adding information, like your voice, to an RF carrier signal.\n\nAM changes the carrier's strength, FM changes its frequency. Demodulation is the reverse, done in the receiver.",
"T7A09":"Some VHF amplifiers have a switch labeled SSB or CW-FM. It sets the amplifier to operate properly in the selected mode.\n\nAn SSB signal's strength rises and falls with your voice, so the amplifier must amplify it faithfully (linearly) and wait a moment before switching back to receive, so it doesn't flip back between words. CW and FM signals stay at one level while on, so the amplifier can run in a simpler, more efficient way. The switch doesn't change your radio's mode; it matches the amplifier's behavior to it.",
"T7A10":"An RF power amplifier takes the transceiver's output and boosts it to a higher power, such as from 10 watts to 100.\n\nA potentiometer only adjusts resistance, and changing impedance doesn't create power, so neither of those helps, and \"all these choices\" is wrong too. To raise transmitted power, add an RF power amplifier.",
"T7A11":"The VFO, variable frequency oscillator, sets your operating frequency. It's what you're adjusting when you turn the tuning knob.\n\nIt controls both receive and transmit frequency.",
"T7B01":"Over-deviating means your voice is swinging the FM signal too far, making it too wide and distorted.\n\nSpeak more softly or hold the mic farther away. That reduces deviation and cleans up your signal.",
"T7B02":"If a broadcast AM or FM radio picks up your transmission, the usual cause is that it can't reject strong signals outside its own band.\n\nYour signal may be perfectly clean and on your own frequency; the receiver simply lacks good filtering. This is called fundamental overload.",
"T7B03":"RFI, radio frequency interference, can come from fundamental overload (a strong signal swamping a receiver), harmonics (multiples of your frequency) or spurious emissions (unwanted signals from a transmitter).\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T7B04":"Solid-state transceivers protect their output transistors. When SWR is high, much of the power reflects back, so the radio automatically lowers its output.\n\nLow power out often means an antenna or feed line problem.",
"T7B05":"When your signal interferes with a neighbor's radio or TV, the fix usually goes on their receiver: a filter at its antenna input that blocks your frequency.\n\nTheir receiver isn't rejecting strong nearby signals, and the filter helps it.",
"T7B06":"When a neighbor reports interference, start at home. Make sure your station is working properly, then tune your own TV or radio to the same channel and transmit.\n\nIf your own set is clean, the problem is likely their equipment. If yours shows interference too, your station needs fixing first. That's the cooperative, fair first step the FCC expects. Hook: check your own house before knocking on theirs.",
"T7B07":"A strong commercial FM broadcast station near 88 to 108 MHz can overload a 2-meter receiver. A band-reject filter (also called a notch filter) blocks that range while letting 2 meters through.\n\nA preamp would boost the interference too and make things worse. Reject the band that's causing trouble.",
"T7B08":"If something in a neighbor's home is interfering with your station, every listed step is right, so the answer is all of these.\n\nWork with your neighbor to identify the offending device. Politely explain the FCC rules, which prohibit devices that cause harmful interference. And make sure your own station meets the standards you're asking of them. Cooperation first, rules second, clean house throughout.",
"T7B09":"Cable TV interference is often caused by loose or poorly installed coax connectors that let RF leak in.\n\nThe first step is to check that every connector is installed properly and tight.",
"T7B10":"Distorted audio through a repeater can come from several things: being off frequency, talking too loudly or too close to the mic, or a poor location with a weak or noisy path.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T7B11":"Sometimes RF from your transmitter travels back down the mic cable and into the radio, distorting your voice.\n\nA clip-on ferrite choke on the mic cable blocks that RF without affecting the audio.",
"T7C01":"A dummy load lets you test a transmitter without sending a signal over the air. It absorbs the power and turns it into heat.\n\nThat avoids interference while you adjust or troubleshoot.",
"T7C02":"An antenna analyzer sweeps frequencies and shows where the antenna is resonant, which is usually close to where SWR is lowest.\n\nIt helps you tune an antenna for the frequency you want.",
"T7C03":"A dummy load is a 50-ohm resistor that doesn't have inductance, mounted on a heat sink.\n\n50 ohms matches the transmitter output. Non-inductive means it looks like a pure resistance at RF. The heat sink gets rid of the heat.",
"T7C04":"SWR, standing wave ratio, shows how well the antenna and feed line are matched. 1:1 means a perfect match, with no power reflected.\n\nUnder 2:1 is usually fine; higher means a mismatch to fix.",
"T7C05":"High SWR reflects power back toward the transmitter. Solid-state output transistors can be damaged by that reflected power.\n\nSo modern radios reduce output automatically as SWR rises, to protect themselves.",
"T7C06":"An SWR of 4:1 means a significant impedance mismatch between the feed line and the antenna. About a third of the power is reflected back toward the transmitter.\n\nSWR is a ratio, not decibels. A tuner or antenna adjustment is needed.",
"T7C07":"Power lost in a feed line is converted into heat. Longer and thinner coax loses more, especially at higher frequencies.\n\nUsing low-loss coax keeps more power going to the antenna.",
"T7C08":"A directional wattmeter measures forward and reflected power. From those two numbers, you can calculate SWR.\n\nSome meters show SWR directly. \"Iambic pentameter\" is the joke answer.",
"T7C09":"Moisture getting into coax is the main way it fails. Water corrodes the shield and changes the dielectric, raising loss.\n\nWeatherproof outdoor connectors carefully.",
"T7C10":"Ultraviolet light from the sun breaks down the plastic jacket on coax, causing cracks.\n\nOnce cracked, water gets in and ruins the cable. UV-resistant jackets last much longer outdoors.",
"T7C11":"Foam-dielectric coax has air bubbles in its insulation, which lowers loss per foot compared with solid dielectric.\n\nThe trade-off is that foam is less rugged and can absorb moisture more easily.",
"T7D01":"A voltmeter measures electric potential, also known as voltage.\n\nVoltage is the difference in electrical potential between two points.",
"T7D02":"A voltmeter connects in parallel, across the component, with one probe on each side.\n\nIt measures the voltage difference between those two points.",
"T7D03":"To measure current, the meter must have the current flow through it, so it goes in series. Break the circuit and insert the meter.\n\nVoltage: parallel. Current: series.",
"T7D04":"An ammeter measures electric current, in amperes. Am-meter: amps meter.\n\nKeep the family straight by unit: voltmeter for volts, ammeter for amps, ohmmeter for ohms, wattmeter for watts. An ammeter connects in series, so the current flows through it.",
"T7D05":"An ohmmeter has its own small battery. It pushes a small, known current through the component and measures the voltage that results.\n\nThen it uses Ohm's law, R = E ÷ I, to show resistance. That's why the circuit must be unpowered: the meter supplies its own current. Hook: the ohmmeter does Ohm's law for you.",
"T7D06":"On the resistance setting, a multimeter supplies its own current. Connecting it to a live voltage source can push current into the meter and damage it.\n\nAlways select the right function before connecting.",
"T7D07":"A multimeter measures several quantities: voltage, resistance and current.\n\nAmong the choices, voltage and resistance is the correct pair.",
"T7D08":"Acid-core solder is for plumbing. The acid is corrosive and will eat away at electronic connections over time.\n\nUse rosin-core solder for electronics.",
"T7D09":"A cold solder joint forms when the solder doesn't get hot enough or the joint moves while cooling. It looks rough, lumpy or dull.\n\nA good joint is smooth and shiny. Cold joints can fail intermittently.",
"T7D10":"An ohmmeter pushes a small current through whatever it touches. A large discharged capacitor starts out empty, so it accepts current easily and the reading starts low.\n\nAs the capacitor charges, current falls, so the reading climbs over time, toward very high resistance. Picture filling a bucket: easy at first, then nothing more goes in. Rising resistance means a capacitor charging.",
"T7D11":"When measuring resistance in a circuit, make sure the circuit isn't powered.\n\nAny voltage present can give false readings or damage the meter, since the ohmmeter supplies its own current.",
"T8A01":"Amplitude modulation changes the strength of a signal to carry information. Single sideband is a refined form of AM.\n\nSSB removes the carrier and one of the two sidebands, keeping only what carries the voice. That makes it narrower and more efficient than plain AM.",
"T8A02":"Packet radio sends data in short bursts. On VHF it usually rides on FM or PM (phase modulation), which are closely related.\n\nThe audio tones carrying the data are fed into an ordinary FM radio, so standard handhelds and mobiles work for packet.",
"T8A03":"For weak-signal, long-distance contacts on VHF and UHF, operators use SSB. It packs all the power into a narrow signal, so it gets through where FM wouldn't.\n\nFM is used for local repeater chat.",
"T8A04":"VHF and UHF repeaters use FM or PM. FM gives clear, noise-free audio with strong signals and is simple to build into radios.\n\nThat's why handhelds and mobiles default to FM.",
"T8A05":"CW (Morse code) is the narrowest of these four, about 150 Hz, because it's just a carrier turned on and off.\n\nSSB voice is about 3 kHz, FM voice 10 to 15 kHz. Slow-scan TV sends still pictures through a voice channel, so it's about 3 kHz too. Don't confuse it with fast-scan TV at 6 MHz.",
"T8A06":"Convention: upper sideband (USB) is used on 10 meters, VHF and UHF. Lower sideband (LSB) is the custom on 40, 80 and 160 meters.\n\nRule of thumb: below 10 MHz, LSB; above, USB. (60 meters is the exception: it uses USB.) Digital modes like FT8 use USB on all bands.",
"T8A07":"SSB is much narrower than FM, about 3 kHz versus 10 to 15 kHz.\n\nThat lets more signals fit in a band and concentrates power, so SSB reaches farther.\n\nIt is not easier to tune: you have to set the frequency closely or voices sound too high or too low, like a cartoon duck.",
"T8A08":"A typical SSB voice signal is about 3 kHz wide, just enough for intelligible speech.\n\nThat's why you need 3 kHz of room from a band edge when operating SSB.",
"T8A09":"FM voice on VHF repeaters is about 10 to 15 kHz wide.\n\nThat's why repeater channels are spaced 15 or 20 kHz apart.",
"T8A10":"Amateur fast-scan TV is about 6 MHz wide, like old analog broadcast TV.\n\nOnly the wide bands at 70 cm and up have room for it.",
"T8A11":"A CW signal needs only about 150 Hz.\n\nIt's just a carrier switched on and off, so it's very narrow. Many CW signals fit where one SSB signal would go.",
"T8A12":"FM has a capture effect: the receiver locks onto the strongest signal and suppresses weaker ones. So you hear only one station at a time.\n\nOn SSB, overlapping signals mix together, so you can hear several at once and pick out one.",
"T8B01":"Satellite beacons send telemetry about the satellite's health and status: battery voltage, temperatures, solar panel currents and so on.\n\nHams decode it to monitor how the satellite is doing.",
"T8B02":"Linear transponder satellites share their limited power among all users. If you transmit too much power, your signal grabs more of it and others get less.\n\nToo much uplink power blocks access by other users.",
"T8B03":"Satellite tracking programs show maps of where satellites are, predict pass times with azimuth and elevation for aiming antennas, and calculate Doppler-corrected frequencies.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T8B04":"Amateur satellites use several modes: SSB and CW on linear transponders, FM on single-channel satellites, and data on others.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T8B05":"A satellite beacon is a transmission from the satellite containing status information, its telemetry.\n\nHearing the beacon also tells you the satellite is in range.",
"T8B06":"Satellite tracking programs use Keplerian elements, a set of numbers describing the satellite's orbit.\n\nThey're updated regularly so predictions stay accurate.",
"T8B07":"Doppler shift is the change in a signal's frequency due to relative motion. As a satellite approaches, its signal appears higher; as it moves away, lower.\n\nLike a passing siren. Operators adjust frequency during a pass to compensate.",
"T8B08":"Satellite mode letters show uplink (you to the satellite) then downlink (satellite to you). U/V means uplink on UHF (70 cm) and downlink on VHF (2 m).\n\nV/U would be the reverse. First letter: up.",
"T8B09":"Spin fading happens because a satellite and its antennas rotate. As the antennas turn, signal strength rises and falls.\n\nIt's a rhythmic fade tied to the satellite's spin.",
"T8B10":"LEO means Low Earth Orbit, a few hundred kilometers up. LEO satellites orbit Earth about every 100 minutes.\n\nThey pass overhead quickly, so contacts are short. The ISS is in LEO.",
"T8B11":"Anyone may receive telemetry from amateur satellites. No license is needed to listen.\n\nAmateur signals may not be encrypted to hide their meaning; only the commands sent up to control a satellite may be. That rules out the encryption-key answers. Many schools decode telemetry for science projects.",
"T8B12":"With a linear transponder, your downlink signal should be about as strong as the satellite's beacon.\n\nIf you're much louder, you're using too much power and crowding others out.",
"T8C01":"Radio direction finding uses directional antennas and receivers to locate a signal's source.\n\nIt's used to track down interference, jammers and lost transmitters.",
"T8C02":"A hidden transmitter hunt, or fox hunt, is a game where hams find a hidden transmitter. A directional antenna is key, showing which direction the signal is strongest.\n\nIt's practice in direction finding.",
"T8C03":"Contesting means making as many contacts as possible within a set time period, often under specific rules.\n\nScores are based on contacts and multipliers like states or countries.",
"T8C04":"In a contest, keep it brief: send only the minimum needed, your call sign and the contest exchange.\n\nLong conversations slow everyone down.",
"T8C05":"A grid locator is a letter-number code for a geographic location, like CM87 for much of the San Francisco Bay Area.\n\nEach square is 1 degree of latitude by 2 degrees of longitude, roughly 70 by 100 miles in the US. VHF and UHF contests and awards often use grid squares.",
"T8C06":"IRLP connects repeaters over the internet. You access a node from your radio by sending DTMF tones (the touch-tones your keypad makes, like a phone).\n\nThe tones dial up the node you want to connect to.",
"T8C07":"VoIP, Voice over Internet Protocol, sends voice over the internet as digital data.\n\nAmateur radio uses VoIP to link repeaters and stations worldwide.",
"T8C08":"IRLP, the Internet Radio Linking Project, connects amateur radio systems like repeaters through the internet.\n\nA ham on one repeater can talk through another repeater across the world.",
"T8C09":"EchoLink lets licensed hams connect to repeaters and other stations using a computer or smartphone, with no radio needed.\n\nThe internet carries your voice to a linked repeater. IRLP, by contrast, is reached only by radio, and D-STAR and DMR are digital radio modes that need a radio.",
"T8C10":"Before using EchoLink, you must register your call sign and provide proof of license.\n\nThat keeps unlicensed people from transmitting through amateur stations.",
"T8C11":"A gateway is an amateur station that connects other amateur stations to the internet, such as an EchoLink or Winlink gateway.\n\nIt bridges radio and the internet.",
"T8D01":"Packet radio, IEEE 802.11 (the technical name for Wi-Fi, which hams adapt for data networks) and FT8 are all digital modes.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T8D02":"FT8 is a digital mode designed to work at very low signal-to-noise ratios. It can decode signals too weak to hear.\n\nExchanges are short and timed in 15-second cycles.",
"T8D03":"APRS, Automatic Packet Reporting System, sends GPS position, text messages, weather data and more.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T8D04":"NTSC is the analog fast-scan color TV standard once used for US broadcast TV.\n\n\"Fast-scan\" means full-motion video like broadcast TV, as opposed to slow-scan still pictures. Hams use it for amateur television on 70 cm and up.",
"T8D05":"APRS supports real-time tactical communications, with stations shown on a map.\n\nIt's useful for public service events and emergencies, tracking people and vehicles.",
"T8D06":"PSK means Phase Shift Keying. Data is sent by shifting the phase of the signal.\n\nPSK31 is a popular narrow digital mode for keyboard chat.",
"T8D07":"DMR, Digital Mobile Radio, uses time slots to fit two digital voice signals in one 12.5 kHz channel. The two take turns, called time-division multiplexing.\n\nThat doubles the capacity of a repeater.",
"T8D08":"Packet radio transmissions include a checksum for error detection, a header with the destination call sign, and automatic repeat requests when errors are found.\n\nAll of the choices are correct.",
"T8D09":"CW, continuous wave, is another name for Morse code transmission. The carrier is switched on and off in dots and dashes.\n\nIt's simple, narrow and effective with weak signals.",
"T8D10":"WSJT-X software supports weak-signal modes for moonbounce (EME), propagation beacons and meteor scatter.\n\nAll of the choices are correct. FT8 is also part of WSJT-X.",
"T8D11":"ARQ, Automatic Repeat reQuest, is an error correction method. When the receiver detects an error, it asks the sender to retransmit.\n\nThat ensures data arrives correctly.",
"T8D12":"An amateur radio mesh network uses commercial Wi-Fi equipment with modified firmware, running on ham frequencies with more power.\n\nIt creates a high-speed data network for hams, useful in emergencies. Broadband-Hamnet and AREDN are examples.",
"T9A01":"A beam antenna concentrates its signal in one direction, like a flashlight instead of a bare bulb. It also hears best in that direction.\n\nA Yagi, the familiar TV-style antenna with several elements, is the classic beam.",
"T9A02":"Loading makes an antenna act electrically longer than it physically is. Inserting a coil (inductor) in the radiating element is the common method.\n\nThat's how short mobile HF antennas resonate on bands that would otherwise need much longer antennas.",
"T9A03":"An antenna's polarization is described by the orientation of its electric field. A vertical antenna is vertically polarized; a horizontal one is horizontally polarized.\n\nMatching polarization between stations gives the best signal on VHF and UHF.",
"T9A04":"The short flexible antenna on a handheld, often called a rubber duck, is compact but inefficient. Much of its energy is lost rather than radiated.\n\nA full-size quarter-wave antenna radiates much better.",
"T9A05":"A dipole's resonant frequency depends on its length. Shortening it raises the frequency; lengthening it lowers it.\n\nShorter antenna, shorter wavelength, higher frequency. Adding coils or capacitive end loading does the opposite: the antenna acts longer and resonates lower.",
"T9A06":"A Yagi has the most gain of the choices because its directors and reflector focus energy in one direction.\n\nGain comes from concentrating signal, not creating power. An isotropic antenna is an imaginary reference that radiates equally in all directions, so it has no gain.",
"T9A07":"A car's metal body acts like a shield. A handheld used inside loses much of its signal strength.\n\nAn external antenna on the roof fixes that.",
"T9A08":"A quarter wave on 2 meters is about 19 inches. That length makes the antenna resonant.\n\nThe band's name gives it away: a quarter of a 2-meter wave is half a meter, roughly 20 inches. (Exactly: 300 / 146 MHz is about 2.05 m; a quarter is about 0.51 m.) Real antennas are trimmed a bit shorter, about 19 inches.",
"T9A09":"A 5/8-wavelength whip has more gain than a 1/4-wave. It concentrates more of the signal toward the horizon, a lower angle, not a higher one.\n\nThat helps reach distant repeaters from a vehicle.",
"T9A10":"A half-wave dipole radiates strongest broadside, at right angles to the wire.\n\nIt sends very little off the ends. Picture a donut with the wire through the hole.",
"T9A11":"Antenna gain is the increase in signal strength in a particular direction compared with a reference antenna.\n\nGain doesn't add power; it focuses existing power. Strength in one direction comes at the expense of others.",
"T9B01":"Every outdoor connector, whatever the type, should be carefully taped or sealed against the weather.\n\nWater getting into a connector ruins the coax. All of the choices are correct.",
"T9B02":"Most amateur coax is 50 ohms, matching the output impedance of most transceivers.\n\nCable TV uses 75-ohm coax, a different standard.",
"T9B03":"Coax is easy to use and needs few special considerations. It can run near metal, be taped to a tower and route through walls.\n\nThat convenience, not low loss or low price, makes it the most common feed line. Open-wire line and hardline have less loss.",
"T9B04":"An antenna tuner, or coupler, matches the antenna system's impedance to the transceiver's output impedance.\n\nThat lets the transmitter deliver full power safely. It doesn't change the antenna itself.",
"T9B05":"Coax loss rises as frequency increases. A cable that's fine on HF may lose much of its signal at UHF.\n\nUse better, lower-loss coax for higher bands.",
"T9B06":"Type N connectors are designed for frequencies above 400 MHz. They keep impedance constant and have a gasket that resists moisture, though you still tape them outdoors.\n\nPL-259 connectors are fine on HF and VHF but not ideal at UHF.",
"T9B07":"PL-259 connectors, often called UHF connectors despite the name, are common at HF and VHF.\n\nThey're inexpensive and easy to install, but they lose performance at higher frequencies. They aren't watertight, and they screw on; the bayonet type is BNC.",
"T9B08":"Coax loses signal from water in connectors, high SWR and extra connectors in the line. With high SWR, reflected power travels back and forth in the cable, losing some on every trip.\n\nEach adds loss, so all of the choices are correct.",
"T9B09":"Erratic SWR changes usually point to a loose connection in the antenna or feed line.\n\nAs it moves, the impedance shifts, and the SWR jumps around. Check and tighten connectors.",
"T9B10":"RG-213 is thicker than RG-58, with a larger center conductor and dielectric. It has less loss at a given frequency.\n\nRG-58 is thinner and lighter but loses more.",
"T9B11":"Air-insulated hardline has the lowest loss. It uses rigid copper with air as most of the dielectric (the insulation between the conductors), and air wastes almost no energy.\n\nIt's expensive and hard to work with, but excellent for long runs at high frequencies.",
"T9B12":"SWR, standing wave ratio, measures how well the load (antenna) is matched to the transmission line (feed line).\n\n1:1 is perfect. Higher numbers mean more reflected power."
};
